  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Tennessee has $11.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 20,286 awards with an average award size of $560K, and obligations are up 3.7% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show a steady pattern: FY21 $10.8B, FY22 $11.4B, FY23 $11.2B, FY24 $10.8B, FY25 $11B, FY26 $11.4B. The Department of Energy is the largest buyer, with $7.4B in obligations, followed by the Department of Defense with $1.6B and the Department of Justice with $726M. CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC and UT-BATTELLE LLC lead vendors with $3.3B and $2.8B respectively. Facilities Support Services dominates by industry with $6.2B, while Remediation Services and Engineering Services account for $733M and $698M. This mix reflects a concentration of large, DOE-related facilities and remediation programs alongside a broader set of services and construction contracts across state federal spending.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting trended in Tennessee?",
        "body": "The state recorded $11.4B in obligations in FY2026 to date, matching the FY22 total of $11.4B. Obligations are up 3.7% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show modest variation: FY21 $10.8B, FY23 $11.2B, FY24 $10.8B, and FY25 $11B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and vendors in Tennessee federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy obligated $7.4B, making it the largest buying agency by a wide margin. The Department of Defense obligated $1.6B and the Department of Justice obligated $726M. CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC and UT-BATTELLE LLC received $3.3B and $2.8B respectively, and Facilities Support Services is the largest industry at $6.2B."
      }
    ]
  },
